.TH PROCESSGROUP 1 "November 2026" "Custom Shell" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
processgroup \- manage a batch of processes: collective signalling and resource monitoring
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B processgroup
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fICOUNT\fR] \fB\-\-\fR \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B processgroup
[\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-f\fR \fIBATCHFILE\fR
.br
.B processgroup
[\fIoptions\fR] \fB\-p\fR \fIPGID\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B processgroup
runs several processes as one Linux process group so they can be signalled
and observed together.
In \fIlaunch mode\fR it starts the processes, places them all in a new group
(\fBsetpgid\fR(2)), optionally monitors their CPU time and resident memory
by reading \fI/proc/<pid>/stat\fR from a supervisor thread
(\fBpthread_create\fR(3)), optionally sends one signal to the entire group with
\fBkill(\-pgid, sig)\fR, waits for every member with \fBwait4\fR(2), and
prints per-process exit status and resource usage.
In \fIattach mode\fR (\fB\-p\fR) it signals and/or monitors a process group
that is already running.
.PP
Interrupting
.B processgroup
(SIGINT or SIGTERM) forwards that signal to the whole group.
A second interrupt sends SIGKILL to the group.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-n " COUNT"
Run \fICOUNT\fR copies of \fICOMMAND\fR in one group (1\-1000, default 1).
.TP
.BI \-f " FILE"
Run every command listed in \fIFILE\fR, one per line, in one group.
Text after \fB#\fR is a comment; blank lines are ignored.
.TP
.BI \-p " PGID"
Attach to existing process group \fIPGID\fR. Requires \fB\-s\fR and/or \fB\-m\fR.
.TP
.BI \-s " SIG"
Signal to send to the whole group, as a name (\fBTERM\fR, \fBSIGKILL\fR,
\fBSTOP\fR, \fBCONT\fR, \fBHUP\fR, \fBINT\fR, \fBQUIT\fR, \fBUSR1\fR,
\fBUSR2\fR, \fBTSTP\fR) or a number.
.TP
.BI \-a " SECS"
Delay before sending the \fB\-s\fR signal. In launch mode the signal is only
sent when \fB\-a\fR is given. In attach mode the signal is sent immediately
unless \fB\-a\fR is given.
.TP
.B \-m
Print a table of PID, parent, state, CPU seconds, resident memory (KB) and
command for the group at every interval.
.TP
.BI \-i " SECS"
Monitoring interval in seconds (may be fractional; default 1).
.TP
.BI \-c " COUNT"
Attach mode: stop after \fICOUNT\fR monitor samples (default: until the group
is empty or interrupted).
.TP
.B \-h
Print usage and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success; in launch mode every process exited with status 0.
.TP
.B 1
Usage error, system call failure, or at least one process failed or was killed
by a signal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Run four sleepers as one group, watch them, and terminate them after 3 seconds:
.PP
.RS
processgroup \-n 4 \-m \-i 0.5 \-s TERM \-a 3 \-\- sleep 60
.RE
.PP
Run a batch file:
.PP
.RS
processgroup \-f batch.txt
.RE
.PP
Pause and resume an existing group (find the PGID with \fBps \-o pgid\fR):
.PP
.RS
processgroup \-p 4242 \-s STOP
.br
processgroup \-p 4242 \-s CONT
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/<pid>/stat
Source of state, CPU time and resident set size.
.SH NOTES
A process that changes its own process group (for example with \fBsetsid\fR(1))
leaves the group and is no longer affected.
\fBSIGKILL\fR and \fBSIGSTOP\fR cannot be caught by members.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kill (2),
.BR setpgid (2),
.BR wait4 (2),
.BR proc (5),
.BR signal (7),
.BR getopt (3)
